\begin{abstract}
The Autonomous Dark Domain Engine (ADDE-v4) introduces a sovereign computational
infrastructure capable of securely ingesting astrophysical survey data,
performing large-scale cosmological simulations, and detecting dark-sector
anomalies in real time. By integrating CKKS homomorphic encryption with
AMR+PM+Octree N-body solvers, ADDE-v4 enables privacy-preserving analysis of
weak-lensing shear fields, BAO correlations, and generative anomaly metrics
across 16{,}384 encrypted SIMD channels. Validation experiments demonstrate
stable $16{,}384^3$ particle evolution, near-peak network saturation, and
automated cryptographic recovery at depth~12. Even under severe observational
conditions ($30\%$ shape noise, $25\%$ masking), ADDE-v4 reconstructs
convergence maps with $\mathrm{SNR}=8.42$ and identifies injected dark-sector
signals at $5.12\sigma$. These results position ADDE-v4 as a next-generation
instrument for probing the dark universe across forthcoming wide-field surveys.
\end{abstract}